editorial_contributions.text:
The first edition of the present book appeared in 2004, [\textit{V. Manturov}, Knot theory. Boca Raton, FL: Chapman \& Hall/CRC (2004; Zbl 1052.57001)]. In a sense, the book is complementary to the two well-known books on classical knot theory by Rolfsen from 1976 and by Burde-Zieschang from 1985 which treat classical knot theory as it has developed together with 3-manifold topology and combinatorial group theory since the beginning of the last century. The field was revolutionized around 1980 by the event of Thurston's geometrization program as well as the discovery of the Jones polynomial, and the main subjects of the present book are the developments and the new invariants following the discovery of the Jones polynomial. The five chapters and some other keywords and highlights are as follows:

I. Knots, links and invariant polynomials (properties of the Jones polynomial; Khovanov's categorification of the Jones polynomial);

II. Theory of braids (Markov's theorem; Yang-Baxter equation);

III. Vassiliev's invariants. Atoms and \(d\)-diagrams (chord diagrams and Feynman diagrams; the Kontsevich integral and formulae for the Vassiliev invariants);

IV. Virtual knots (polynomials and Khovanov homology for virtual knots);

V. Knots, 3-manifolds, and Legendrian knots (Witten-Reshetikhin-Turaev invariants; Heegaard-Floer homology).

Among the topics added to the first edition there are Heegaard Floer homology, the Rasmussen invariant, Khovanov homology for virtual knots, the \(A\)-polynomial, and the solution to the braid conjugacy problem. The book augmented from the 400 pages of the first edition to the present 560 pages and contains a wealth of information on various recent developments of knot theory and knot invariants (with the exception of the geometrization after Thurston, hyperbolic knots, volumes etc). It starts with a nice and somewhat enigmatic poem by Hermann Hesse on the role of signs and symbols which play such an eminent role in the formalisms of recent knot invariants (and in a more and more virtual world).
